      "title": "Episode 330\n                Disaster Recovery for Executives: Why Cloud & SaaS Are NOT Enough",
      "summary": "Episode 330\n                Disaster Recovery for Executives: Why Cloud & SaaS Are NOT Enough\n            \n\n            \n                \n                    3/3/2026\n                \n                \n          \n          Hosted by Dr. Darren Pulsipher\n        \n            \n            \n            \n        \n\n        \n\n        \n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                    \n                \n            \n\n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                    \n\n                    \n                        Explore more in the episode archive.\n                    \n                \n            \n\n            \n                Coming Soon...Come back on 3/3/2026to see and listen to this amazing episode\n            \n        \n\n        \n            (function () {\n                const mediaRoot = document.getElementById('episode-media');\n                if (!mediaRoot) return;\n\n                const releaseDateValue = mediaRoot.getAttribute('data-release-date');\n                const releaseDate = releaseDateValue ? new Date(releaseDateValue) : null;\n\n                const video = mediaRoot.querySelector('.video-container');\n                const audio = mediaRoot.querySelector('.podcast-container');\n                const comingSoon = document.getElementById('coming-soon');\n\n                if (!releaseDate || isNaN(releaseDate.getTime())) {\n                    if (comingSoon) comingSoon.hidden = false;\n                    return;\n                }\n\n                const today = new Date();\n                today.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);\n                releaseDate.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0);\n\n                const isReleased = releaseDate <= today;\n\n                if (video) video.hidden = !isReleased;\n                if (audio) audio.hidden = !isReleased;\n                if (comingSoon) comingSoon.hidden = isReleased;\n            })();\n        \n\n        \n            Summary\n            Disaster recovery is a crucial yet often overlooked aspect of business continuity. Join Dr. Darren as he converses with Tom May, CEO of Different Dev, about why cloud solutions and Software as a Service (SaaS) aren’t enough for comprehensive disaster recovery. Discover actionable insights that can h\n\n\n            \n\n            \n            \n                \n                \n              \n                Tom May\n              \n            \n                \n                \n              \n                Darren W Pulsipher\n              \n            \n                \n            \n            \n        \n\n        \n            Preparing for the Unexpected: Insights from Disaster Recovery Experts\nIn today’s fast-paced digital landscape, business leaders must prioritize disaster recovery. With numerous unexpected events, such as natural disasters, system failures, and cyber threats, a robust disaster recovery (DR) strategy is essential. Dr. Darren, a leading voice in the field, is joined by Tom May, an expert in disaster recovery and CEO of Different Dev, to share pressing insights and strategies for creating effective disaster recovery plans that truly safeguard organizations.\nTom’s unique perspective on disaster recovery stems from his rich experience in IT and the recognition that businesses often overlook critical aspects until it's too late. His knowledge serves as a wake-up call for executives at all levels, underscoring the importance of being prepared in the digital age.\nUnderstanding the Basics of Disaster Recovery\nWhat is Disaster Recovery?\nAt its core, disaster recovery is the practice of preparing for potential disastrous events that can disrupt business operations. This includes developing systems and processes. The goal is to minimize downtime and data loss, ensuring business continuity. \nFor many organizations, a common misconception is that cloud solutions or Software as a Service (SaaS) alone provide adequate disaster recovery. However, Tom highlights how reliance on these tools can foster a false sense of security. While they provide certain back-end solutions, they do not replace a comprehensive disaster recovery strategy tailored to a business's specific needs.\nKey Components of a Strong Disaster Recovery Plan\nA well-rounded disaster recovery plan includes:\n\nRisk Assessment: Understand and evaluate the potential threats to your organization’s information and system integrity.\n\nBusiness Impact Analysis: Identify critical operations and prioritize which systems need to be restored first.\n\nRecovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO): Establish clear metrics for how quickly systems must be restored and how much data can be affordably lost.\n\nContinuous Testing and Updating: Regularly test your disaster recovery plan and update it as new systems and technologies are implemented.\n\n\nKey Takeaways:\n\nDisaster recovery is essential for business continuity amidst unexpected disruptions.\n\nRelying solely on cloud solutions can lead to gaps in preparedness.\n\nRegular testing of your disaster recovery plans is crucial.\n\n\nThe Executive’s Role in Robust Disaster Recovery\nPrioritizing Disaster Recovery in Business Strategy\nFor executives, understanding the necessity of a structured disaster recovery plan is vital. Many organizations mistakenly believe they are protected, which can lead to unexpected vulnerabilities. As Tom emphasized, discerning the nuances of what a business actually needs depends on a clear assessment of risk versus cost.\nExecutives should also ensure that disaster recovery budgeting is treated as a long-term investment rather than a one-time cost. It's crucial to foster a culture that acknowledges potential threats and the importance of preventive action as an integral part of the organizational strategy.\nBuilding a Culture of Preparedness\nCreating a disaster recovery plan is just the beginning. Organizations must cultivate a culture where all employees—including IT and security teams—understand the significance of their roles in ensuring business continuity. Regular training and simulations help reinforce this culture and prepare the workforce for real crises.\nActionable Steps for Executives:\n\nInvest in professional training for disaster recovery and crisis management.\n\nEngage with experts to assess your current disaster recovery plans.\n\nRegularly review and update recovery strategies based on technological advancements.\n\n\nWhy You Can’t Afford to Wait: Take Action Now\nThe reality of digital transformation is that it requires constant vigilance. Organizations cannot afford to wait until a disaster strikes to develop their disaster recovery plans. Instead, they need to proactively ensure they have the right systems in place to mitigate risks. If you found insights from this discussion valuable, now is the time to assess your organization's disaster recovery strategy.\nExplore how your organization can enhance its disaster recovery capabilities by consulting experts like Tom May or leveraging resources to build a tailored strategy. Don't leave your future to chance; take action today to secure your organization's tomorrow.\nBy taking these steps, you not only safeguard your business but also foster a resilient operational environment that thrives even in the face of adversity. \n\nIf you want to dive deeper into the concepts shared in this blog and hear from Tom May, be sure to check out the full podcast episode where these insights were discussed in greater depth. For more valuable resources and community support related to digital transformation, visit Embracing Digital. Stay informed, and let's keep the conversation going!",
